  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 119 AM CDT Wed May 13 2026\n\nLight to calm southeasterly winds and mostly clear skies will a \npromote favorable fog environment over the inland Coastal Plains \nthis morning. There is a medium to high chance (40-70%) for areas of \ndense fog to develop, mainly over the Victoria Crossroads. We can \nexpect similar conditions early Thursday morning as well. \n\nMid-level ridging will dominate going into this weekend, continuing \nnear to slightly above normal temperatures and rain-free conditions \nacross South Texas. Highs will consistently range from the mid 80s \nalong the coast to the mid 90s over the Rio Grande Plains through \nthis weekend.\n\nLow to medium rain chances (20-40%) return early next week as PWATs \nnear 2.0\" (99th percentile) in conjunction with embedded mid-level \nshortwaves and a cold front pushing southward across Texas. Pacific \nmoisture will usher in as a deep trough sags south over the western \nCONUS, meanwhile 925mb Mean Specific Humidity nears the \nclimatological max. This combination will result in not just \nincreased cloud cover and rain chances, but extremely warm dewpoints \nand minimum temperatures. We're looking at multiple soupy mornings \nflirting with record high minimum temperatures around 80 degrees \nfrom Sunday morning to Wednesday morning. Due to the lack of heat \nrecovery at night and max heat indices 100-110, there is a moderate \nrisk of heat-related impacts during the aforementioned period.",
